# Concepts and metrics

## Short glossary

- Read: a short piece of DNA sequence produced by the sequencer. Short reads are about 50 to 300 bp, long reads 10,000 bp or more.
- Contig: overlapping sequences merged into one continuous stretch.
- Scaffold: contigs joined in order with gaps of unknown sequence between them.
- Coverage (depth): how many reads cover a given position.
- k-mer: a subsequence of length k used to estimate coverage and genome size without an assembly.
- Masking: hiding repetitive regions so gene prediction does not make mistakes there.
- Annotation: locating genes and regulatory regions and assigning functions to them.
- Paired-end reads (R1 and R2): both ends of a fragment are sequenced, which helps place reads during assembly.
- De novo assembly: built from the reads alone. Costly, but it keeps what is unique to the organism. Reference-guided assembly is faster but depends on the quality of the reference and can miss genes the reference lacks.

## Sequencing technologies in this workshop

| | Illumina | Nanopore / PacBio |
|---|---|---|
| Read length | short, around 150 bp | very long, 10 kb or more |
| Cost per read | low, very many reads | higher, fewer reads |
| Error rate | low | higher |
| Repeats | cannot span long repeats | can resolve long repeats |

Eukaryotic genomes have many repeats, so Illumina-only assemblies tend to be fragmented. Combining both gives contiguity and accuracy.

## Quality scores

Phred score Q relates to error probability: Q20 is a 1% chance of error, Q30 is 0.1%. In FASTQ each score is stored as one ASCII character, for example ":" corresponds to 25.

Thresholds mentioned in the workshop:
- Illumina: Q20 or higher is acceptable
- Nanopore and PacBio: above Q7 is acceptable, because there are fewer reads and a higher error rate is tolerated

